---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const recipes = (await getCollection('recipes')).filter(
  (recipe) => !['no-cook-playdough', 'play-dough'].includes(recipe.slug)
);
const reviewed = recipes.filter(
  (r) => r.data.learning?.review.status === 'editorial-review'
).length;
const tested = recipes.filter((r) => r.data.learning?.review.status === 'kitchen-tested').length;
const meals = await getCollection('meals');
const reviewedMeals = meals.filter(
  (meal) => meal.data.review?.status === 'editorial-review'
).length;
---

<Layout
  title="Our recipe standard | Mise"
  description="How Mise develops, reviews, and teaches recipes, and what editorial review and kitchen testing actually mean."
>
  <div class="standards prose-refined">
    <p class="eyebrow">Our recipe standard</p><h1>Good recipes earn your trust.</h1><p>
      Mise is growing from a family recipe collection into a kitchen that teaches. That means being
      specific about what works, explaining why, and being honest about what has—and has not—been
      tested.
    </p>
    <div class="review-counts">
      <div><strong>{recipes.length}</strong><span>recipes in the collection</span></div><div>
        <strong>{reviewed}</strong><span>with detailed editorial review</span>
      </div><div><strong>{tested}</strong><span>with recorded kitchen tests</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      {reviewedMeals} of {meals.length} composed meals have also received an individual review of portions,
      components, and cooking order. These are editorial reviews, not recorded whole-meal kitchen tests.
    </p>
    <h2>What every recipe should teach</h2><ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The intended result.</strong> Describe flavor, texture, and what success looks like before
        cooking starts.
      </li><li>
        <strong>The important decisions.</strong> Connect a technique to a reason and an observable cue.
        Explain when the clock is only an estimate.
      </li><li>
        <strong>A reproducible method.</strong> Specify meaningful weights, cuts, pan sizes, temperatures,
        sequencing, and total elapsed time, including rests and preparation.
      </li><li>
        <strong>How to adapt.</strong> Explain the effect of a substitution and when a different ingredient
        needs a different method.
      </li><li>
        <strong>How to recover.</strong> Identify likely causes, useful fixes, and changes that cannot
        be reversed.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Context and evidence.</strong> Respect the dish’s origins, preserve family attribution,
        and keep sources for scientific and safety claims.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <h2>How review and testing differ</h2><h3>In the collection</h3><p>
      An existing recipe, preserved with its family and source context. Detailed technique review
      and testing may still be outstanding. Availability on the site is not a testing certification.
    </p><h3>Editorially reviewed</h3><p>
      The written recipe has been checked for internal consistency, method, quantities, timing,
      technique explanations, and relevant sources. It has not necessarily been cooked. A review
      date records an editorial revision, not independent verification.
    </p><h3>Kitchen-tested</h3><p>
      Reserved for a documented cook: ingredient weights and brands where relevant, equipment,
      actual timings, measured temperatures, yield, sensory results, and any changes. Repeated tests
      and a second cook strengthen confidence. We do not assign this label from automated checks or
      generated prose.
    </p>
    <h2>Science serves the food</h2><p>
      A chemical mechanism explains a useful decision; it is not decoration. “Starch helps stabilize
      this sauce” should lead to reserving water, controlling heat, and recognizing a glossy
      coating. Traditions differ, so a technique that helps one dish is not a universal rule for
      every cuisine.
    </p>
    <h2>Measurements and nutrition</h2><p>
      Weights make variable ingredients such as grated cheese more reproducible. Portion controls
      scale ingredient amounts; they do not automatically scale cookware, cooking times, or every
      quantity mentioned in the method. Legacy nutrition estimates are withheld from recipe pages
      while ingredient-based calculations and yields are reviewed.
    </p>
    <h2>Sources and helpful references</h2><p>
      We retain recipe-specific references and review records as part of our editorial work.
      Original recipe authors are credited where their recipes are adapted. Review dates and
      supporting references describe the work behind a recipe; they do not document a kitchen test.
      Practical instructions stay with the recipe, while this page explains our process.
    </p><ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/keep-food-safe/4-steps-to-food-safety"
          >FoodSafety.gov: clean, separate, cook and chill</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/safe-minimum-internal-temperatures"
          >Cooking and reheating temperatures</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/selecting-and-serving-fresh-and-frozen-seafood-safely"
          >FDA guidance for choosing and preparing seafood</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/refrigerator-thermometers-cold-facts-about-food-safety"
          >Refrigerator temperatures and food storage</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>How we improve the collection</h2><p>
      Automated checks identify missing teaching fields, inconsistent timing, broken references, and
      claims that need review across the whole collection. They create an editorial queue; passing
      them does not prove a recipe tastes good. Each recipe still needs individual attention and
      real cooking evidence.
    </p>
    <p><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}learn`}>Explore the learning kitchen →</a></p>
  </div>
</Layout>
<style>
  .standards {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: auto;
  }
  h1 {
    font:
      500 clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.4rem)/1.1 'Cormorant Garamond',
      Georgia,
      serif;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }
  .review-counts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1.2rem;
    border-block: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }
  .review-counts strong {
    display: block;
    font:
      500 2.6rem/1 'Cormorant Garamond',
      Georgia,
      serif;
    color: var(--accent-gold);
  }
  .review-counts span {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }
</style>
